.TH MOUNT 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount \- mount a filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount
.I device
.I dir
.I type
.br
.B mount
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR options ]
.B \-t
.I type
.I device
.I dir
.br
.B mount
.B \-o remount\c
.RI [, options ]
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount
attaches the filesystem of type
.I type
found on
.I device
to the directory
.I dir
using
.BR mount (2).
The first form, with three positional operands, is the traditional
Substrate syntax; the second form takes the type from
.BR \-t .
With
.BR \-t ,
exactly two operands are required; without it, exactly three.
.PP
.I device
is normally a block device under
.IR /dev/storage/ ,
named by driver type and instance with an optional partition suffix,
for example
.IR /dev/storage/ide0 ,
.IR /dev/storage/sata0p2 ,
or
.IR /dev/storage/virtio0 .
It may also be given as
.BI LABEL= name\fR,
in which case the kernel scans every block device for a filesystem
with that volume label and mounts the first match.
Filesystems that need no device accept any placeholder word, such as
.BR none .
.PP
.I type
must name a filesystem registered in the kernel.
The in-tree kernel provides
.BR ext2 ,
.BR fat ,
.BR exfat ,
.BR udf ,
.BR minix ,
.BR sysv ,
.BR xenix ,
.BR 9p ,
.BR fuse ,
.BR shmfs ,
.BR procfs ,
.BR sysfs ,
and
.BR devfs .
.PP
Only the superuser may mount filesystems.
The kernel refuses to mount anything over
.IR /dev ,
.IR /proc ,
or
.I /sys
(except as a remount), and refuses a second mount on
.IR / .
The kernel itself mounts the root filesystem and then
.B devfs
on
.IR /dev ,
.B shmfs
on
.IR /dev/shm ,
.B procfs
on
.IR /proc ,
and
.B sysfs
on
.I /sys
at boot, so these never need to be mounted by hand.
.PP
.B mount
does not read
.I /etc/fstab
and does not maintain
.IR /etc/mtab .
Run with no operands it prints a usage message; it does not list the
current mounts.
.SS Remounting
If
.I options
contains
.BR remount ,
the filesystem already mounted on
.I dir
has its flags changed in place, typically to switch between
read-only and read-write.
Only
.I dir
is required; a second operand, if given, is passed as the source but
is not otherwise used, and
.B \-t
is optional.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " type"
Filesystem type.
.TP
.B \-r
Mount read-only.
Applied after
.BR \-o ,
so it overrides an
.B rw
there.
.TP
.BI \-o " options"
A comma-separated list of mount options.
The following are translated into
.BR mount (2)
flags:
.RS
.TP
.B ro
Read-only.
.TP
.B rw
Read-write; cancels an earlier
.BR ro .
.TP
.B remount
Change the flags of an existing mount (see
.BR Remounting ).
.TP
.B nosuid
Ignore set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits.
.TP
.B nodev
Do not interpret device special files.
.TP
.B noexec
Do not allow execution of programs.
.TP
.B sync
Synchronous I/O.
.TP
.B async
Asynchronous I/O.
.TP
.B defaults
Accepted and ignored.
.RE
.IP
Other words are not rejected.
The complete option string is passed to
.BR mount (2)
as its
.I data
argument, but see BUGS.
The kernel rejects
.B async
combined with
.B ro
or
.BR sync .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The filesystem was mounted.
.TP
.B 1
The command line was invalid, or
.BR mount (2)
failed.
In the latter case a message naming the device, directory, type and
error is written to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/storage/
Block devices.
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
Filesystem table used by the boot scripts.
Each entry lists, in order, the device (usually as
.BR LABEL= ),
the mount point, the type, the options, and the dump and pass
numbers; the first three fields are the operands of the traditional
.B mount
form.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount the second partition of the first SATA disk on
.IR /mnt :
.PP
.EX
# mount /dev/storage/sata0p2 /mnt ext2
.EE
.PP
Mount a FAT filesystem by volume label, read-only:
.PP
.EX
# mount \-r \-t fat LABEL=sub\-boot /boot
.EE
.PP
Make the root filesystem writable after checking it:
.PP
.EX
# mount \-o remount,rw /
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR umount (8),
.BR mount (2),
.BR umount (2),
.BR ext2 (4),
.BR proc (5),
.BR rootfs (7)
.SH BUGS
When
.I device
names a node under
.IR /dev ,
the kernel passes that device to the filesystem in place of the
.I data
argument, so filesystem-specific
.B \-o
options are only seen by filesystems mounted without a device.
.PP
Only the first 255 bytes of
.I options
are examined for the flags listed above.
